From: postmarketOS <bramble@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] net: allow SO_BINDTODEVICE on a socket that is not bound yet, without CAP_NET_RAW

Mainline has allowed this since 5.7 (commit c427bfec18f2, "net: core: enable
SO_BINDTODEVICE for non-root users"). A process can bind a socket that is
not bound to a device yet. Only a change of an existing binding, or the
removal of it, needs CAP_NET_RAW.

This kernel still has the old check in sock_bindtoindex_locked(), so every
SO_BINDTODEVICE from an ordinary user fails with EPERM. mmsd-tng binds its
DNS queries and its HTTP requests to the interface of the MMS bearer. With
the EPERM, the DNS queries left on Wi-Fi and timed out, and no MMS could
be downloaded or sent. Programs written for a current kernel assume that
this works, so fix the kernel instead of giving each program a capability.

---
--- a/net/core/sock.c
+++ b/net/core/sock.c
@@ -571,7 +571,7 @@
 
 	/* Sorry... */
 	ret = -EPERM;
-	if (!ns_capable(net->user_ns, CAP_NET_RAW))
+	if (sk->sk_bound_dev_if && !ns_capable(net->user_ns, CAP_NET_RAW))
 		goto out;
 
 	ret = -EINVAL;
